      en: "Monte Alén National Park is the cornerstone of conservation efforts on the mainland of Equatorial Guinea, protecting one of the most biodiverse regions in Central Africa. Spanning over 2,000 square kilometers, the park consists of a vast complex of dense tropical primary rainforest in the Rio Muni region. The park's rugged, hilly topography and numerous rivers create a dynamic and resilient ecosystem that provides a vital habitat for spectacular wildlife. This includes endangered species such as forest elephants, western lowland gorillas, and chimpanzees. Established in 1997, the park serves as a critical sanctuary for the preservation of regional biodiversity and is a high priority for international environmental research. Monte Alén is also a developing site for sustainable ecotourism, offering guided treks through some of the continent's most pristine wilderness areas.",
